/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"production-level-1","number":1,"title":"Module 1 — Job, Form, and Hygiene","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"A producer in this course is someone who finishes sectional music inside Ableton Live. You are not “making beats” as an identity. You are not “designing a preset.” You decide where sections sit, what contrasts them, and when the piece is ov","modules":[{"id":"production-m-1-1","code":"1","title":"Part A: The Job, the Six Stages, the Loop Trap","blurb":"A producer in this course is someone who finishes sectional music inside Ableton Live. You are not “making beats” as an identity. You are not “designing a preset.” You decide where sections sit, what contrasts them, and when the piece is ov","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 1 — Part A: The Job, the Six Stages, the Loop Trap","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Define what a producer in <em>this</em> school finishes. Separate six production jobs. Leave an 8-bar cell on purpose.   <strong>Required.</strong> No gate.   <strong>Do not:</strong> open Vital, design a drop synth, tour Suite.   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> <em>Music-Production-Songwriting-Knowledgebase</em> §1; OUR GOAL §3E / §6.7; EDMProd <em>Loop Trap Cheat Sheet</em> + <em>How to Avoid The 8-Bar Loop Trap</em> (Matla); <em>3 Ways to Write a Drop</em>; <em>Finish Tracks in Ableton Live Faster Through Commitment</em>. The EDMProd “5 Stages of Every Producer” article is a <em>career</em> roadmap (Initiation → Mastery). It is <strong>not</strong> the six production jobs. Do not mix the two lists."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 1.1 — The job","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Tell a loop from a song. Name the four deliverables. Know which sister course owns what."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-1","title":"1.1.1 — A song is not an 8-bar loop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.1 — A song is not an 8-bar loop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Why an 8-bar groove is a cell, not a finished piece.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A producer in this course is someone who <strong>finishes sectional music inside Ableton Live</strong>. You are not “making beats” as an identity. You are not “designing a preset.” You decide where sections sit, what contrasts them, and when the piece is over."},{"type":"prose","html":"An 8-bar loop can be excellent. House, techno, and a lot of hip-hop <em>live</em> on repeated cells. Beat Kitchen will later say repetition is a feature of electronic music, not a bug. The problem is <strong>never leaving the cell</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sam Matla (EDMProd, <em>How to Avoid The 8-Bar Loop Trap</em>): it is easy to make a loop; it is hard to make a structured song. A 4-bar, 8-bar, even 16-bar idea that never grows is still a loop. If you only ever finish loops, you only ever practice the first 10% of the job — drums, a bass, a nice sound — and you never practice tension, section contrast, or the last 10% of a project."},{"type":"prose","html":"Local KB §1.2: a finished <strong>90-second</strong> loop-based track teaches more than a perfect half-finished epic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Cell vs song (this school’s test):</strong>"},{"type":"table","headers":["If you have…","You have…"],"rows":[["One repeating idea, no named neighbor","A <strong>cell</strong>"],["At least two sections that a listener can point at (verse/chorus, or build/drop, or add/subtract)","The start of a <strong>song</strong>"],["A start, a change, and an ending you chose","A <strong>piece</strong> you can export"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can you say “cell” without insulting loops?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-2","title":"1.1.2 — Sketch vs demo vs deliverable","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.2 — Sketch vs demo vs deliverable","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Name the difference so you stop polishing a sketch as if it were a release.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Three words this course will keep separate:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Word","Meaning here"],"rows":[["<strong>Sketch</strong>","An idea you are still allowed to throw away. Capture MIDI, a hummed hook, four unnamed clips."],["<strong>Demo</strong>","A listen-through of a <em>shape</em>. Sections exist. It may be ugly. Someone else could tell where the chorus is."],["<strong>Deliverable</strong>","A file you would hand a teacher: named sections, exported bounce, versioned Project. Not mixed-for-Spotify. <em>Finished enough.</em>"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Beginners injure themselves by mixing a sketch (KB §1.1: mixing while still rewriting the chorus). Treat the three words as permission: a sketch may stay ugly; a deliverable may not stay nameless."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Point at one abandoned idea on your drive and say which of the three it actually is."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-3","title":"1.1.3 — Four deliverables, four mindsets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.3 — Four deliverables, four mindsets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Session song, Arrangement song, remix, and flip — four files, not one idea four ways.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"YOUR GOAL / academy spec §3E and §6.7 lock <strong>three sections</strong> of this course:"},{"type":"list","items":["Session-view songwriting  ","Arrangement-view songwriting  ","Remix / flip of an existing song  "],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"You later locked a fourth <em>file</em>: remix <strong>and</strong> flip are both required. So the school asks for <strong>four pieces</strong>:"},{"type":"table","headers":["File","Mindset","Finished means"],"rows":[["<code>Session_v01</code>","Non-linear clips and scenes","A complete song <strong>performed from scenes</strong>"],["<code>Arrangement_v01</code>","Linear ruler","A <strong>different</strong> complete song written left-to-right"],["<code>Remix_v01</code>","Transform a record; keep a key element","Still recognizably that song"],["<code>Flip_v01</code>","Chop until it may not be that song","New identity; still a legal source"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton’s manual (ch. 7.5) is explicit: Session clips and Arrangement clips <strong>exist independently</strong>. Recording a jam onto the ruler is a <em>log of that pass</em>, not permission to submit one song twice."},{"type":"prose","html":"Industry habit (Music City SF / Soundfly): sketch in Session, polish in Arrangement. This course <strong>teaches that habit as history of a jam</strong>, then requires you to finish one song <em>in Session on purpose</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Practice (do this now):</strong> Create four empty notes or folders:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Session_v01</code> · <code>Arrangement_v01</code> · <code>Remix_v01</code> · <code>Flip_v01</code>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Why is remix the third <em>section</em> but the third <em>and</em> fourth <em>file</em>?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-4","title":"1.1.4 — Why a remix is not “song 1 with a new kick”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.4 — Why a remix is not “song 1 with a new kick”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A remix starts from someone else’s record. Song 1 is yours.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A remix (EDMProd, Matla) balances familiarity with something new. You typically keep a <strong>key element</strong> — usually the vocal, otherwise the bass or the lead — or listeners will not identify it as a remix. If you delete that element, you wrote an original that happens to share a folder with someone else’s stems."},{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>flip</strong>, in this school, treats the source as raw material. Identity may disappear. Copyright does <strong>not</strong>. Both paths require a legal / owned / royalty-free source. Suite Stem Separation is not a student step."},{"type":"prose","html":"Song 1 (<code>Session_v01</code>) is <em>your</em> sectional music. Putting a new kick under it is arrangement, not a remix."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> In one sentence each: remix, flip, original."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-5","title":"1.1.5 — Sister courses — what we will not teach","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.5 — Sister courses — what we will not teach","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Ableton, Drums, Sound Design, DJ’ing, and Launchkey each own a slice. This course points, it does not steal.  "},{"type":"table","headers":["Course","Owns","This course does"],"rows":[["<strong>Mastering Ableton Live 12</strong>","Device encyclopedia, warp-mode footnotes, Operator/M4L as Live tools","Uses Session, Arrangement, warp, slice at <em>producer</em> depth"],["<strong>Learning the Launchkey MK4 61</strong>","Components, every pad mode, Windows MIDI, standalone","Capture, Clip mode, 16-pad chops, transport"],["<strong>Drums &amp; Rhythm Programming</strong>","Kick/snare science, Amen edits, 32nd hats","Places drums as <em>section contrast</em>"],["<strong>Sound Design Foundations</strong>","Synthesis, Vital / Massive X / Surge, envelopes as design","Default sounds and stock devices are legal finishers"],["<strong>Foundations of DJ'ing</strong>","Beatmatch, two-deck phrasing, set architecture","Names DJ intro / outro as <em>form</em>, does not blend two records"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"You own Operator and Max for Live. <strong>Do not open them for homework here.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name two things you will <em>not</em> do in this course, and which sister owns them."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-6","title":"1.1.6 — Genre labs skip — how and why","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.6 — Genre labs skip — how and why","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Every genre lab is skippable. Pop form is always there if you skip them all.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Modules 3–6 and 9–10 are <strong>genre labs</strong>. In the later app they will be marked <code>{ optional: true }</code> — <strong>Genre lab — skip anytime.</strong> No gate."},{"type":"prose","html":"Why they exist: Beat Kitchen’s warning. Sitting down to “make electronic music” with no stylistic target often produces a house tempo, a dubstep bass, and trap hats. Genre is a <strong>starting coordinate</strong>, not a cage."},{"type":"prose","html":"Why they skip: you asked for many genres, one at a time, without locking the spine to all of them. Capstones use a genre you <strong>opened</strong>, or <strong>pop form</strong>, which is always taught in this Module."},{"type":"prose","html":"Light labs (lo-fi, reggae/dub, ambient, country, extra Arrangement cousins) stay in the book. They are not required."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> If you skip every lab, what form do the capstones still have?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-7","title":"1.1.7 — Finish-small: 90-second songs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.7 — Finish-small: 90-second songs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A finished 90-second piece teaches more than an abandoned epic.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Local KB §1.2, principle 1: <strong>Finish small songs.</strong> A 90-second loop-based track teaches more than a perfect half-finished epic."},{"type":"prose","html":"Later, KB §9.7 gives a first complete-song recipe aimed at <strong>2–2.5 minutes</strong>. Those are two different targets. Early homework may be 90 seconds. Capstones may grow. Neither number is a law of physics."},{"type":"prose","html":"Matla’s reason to finish (loop-trap article): if you never get past step one you never learn tension, energy, or the last 10% of a project. Finishing <em>small</em> is how you get past step one without lying to yourself about a 7-minute techno epic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Would you rather export 90 honest seconds tonight, or keep an unnamed 8-bar forever?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-1-8","title":"1.1.8 — One new skill per session","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.8 — One new skill per session","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Pick one craft for the hour. Do not open a synth because you are stuck on form.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.2, principle 2: <strong>One new skill per session.</strong> Example given there: “Today I only learn Session View clips.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"If the hour’s skill is “name six scenes,” opening Vital is not courage. It is avoidance. Matla: we stay in the loop because we do the comfortable work (kicks, another layer) and postpone the uncomfortable work (a second section, a melody, a decision)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Also from KB §1.2: <strong>default sounds are fine.</strong> Core Library first."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.1 practice:</strong> The four titled files from 0.1.3.   <strong>Section 1.1 checkpoint:</strong> Remix ≠ “song 1 with a new kick.” Flip ≠ remix."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-1","title":"1.2.1 — Write — melody, chords, lyrics, form","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.1 — Write — melody, chords, lyrics, form","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The first job: decide what the sections are before you mix them.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.1:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Goal","Output"],"rows":[["<strong>Write</strong>","Melody, chords, lyrics, form","Demo idea (even rough)"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Writing, in this school, includes <strong>form</strong>. An 8-bar chord loop with no named neighbor is not done writing. You do not need finished lyrics. You do need to know whether the next cell is a verse, a chorus, a build, or a breakdown."},{"type":"prose","html":"Instrumental writers: treat the lead like a lyric — phrases and breaths (KB §8, just above the form table)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> What is the <em>output</em> of Write, according to the table — a mix, or a demo idea?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-2","title":"1.2.2 — Produce / arrange — instruments, groove, section contrast","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.2 — Produce / arrange — instruments, groove, section contrast","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The second job: make verse and chorus feel like different rooms.  "},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Goal","Output"],"rows":[["<strong>Produce / arrange</strong>","Instruments, groove, section contrast","Full arrangement"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the job the rest of the course lives in. Contrast is not “louder.” KB §9.4 will later demand <strong>at least two</strong> of: more/fewer instruments, higher register, brighter hats, careful width, harmonic lift, a fill into the downbeat, denser or more locked bass."},{"type":"prose","html":"You will often loop <strong>Write → Arrange → Record</strong>. KB says that is normal."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name the output of this stage without saying “it sounds pro.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-3","title":"1.2.3 — Record — MIDI and audio as capture, not as “the mix”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.3 — Record — MIDI and audio as capture, not as “the mix”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Press record to catch a take. Mixing comes later.  "},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Goal","Output"],"rows":[["<strong>Record</strong>","Capture performances (MIDI + audio)","Clean takes"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Record is a <strong>capture</strong> job. Session Record into a slot, Capture MIDI after the fact, Arrangement Record of a long take — all of that is Record. It is not an invitation to insert Ozone."},{"type":"prose","html":"MK4: hardware Record follows the <strong>active view</strong> (KB §5.5). Session View → Session Record. Arrangement View → Arrangement Record. Know which window is in front."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> If you are still rewriting the chords, are you recording or still writing?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-4","title":"1.2.4 — Edit — timing, cleanup, section lengths","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.4 — Edit — timing, cleanup, section lengths","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Tighten what you captured. Do not rewrite the chorus in the mix window.  "},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Goal","Output"],"rows":[["<strong>Edit</strong>","Timing, pitch, cleanup","Tight, listen-able arrangement"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Edit includes section lengths. Shortening a 32-bar verse because it bores you is <strong>Edit / arrange</strong>, not mixing. Comping a vocal (Live ch. 21, later) is Edit."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pitch correction exists. It is not this course’s degree. Point at Ableton / later finishing if you need it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Is “the verse is 8 bars too long” a mix problem?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-5","title":"1.2.5 — Mix — named, then pointed at later finishing habits","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.5 — Mix — named, then pointed at later finishing habits","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> This course names mixing so you do not confuse it with arranging.  "},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Goal","Output"],"rows":[["<strong>Mix</strong>","Balance, space, clarity","Stereo mix"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"We <strong>name</strong> Mix so you stop using the word for everything. Compressor ratios, bus processing, and “pro loudness” are <strong>not</strong> this course. Module 14 will ask you to listen twice (form first, then noise) and keep a reference lane at low volume (KB §1.2). That is finishing hygiene, not a mixing school."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustNotCover:</strong> threshold / ratio homework."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can you describe Mix in the table’s three words only?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-6","title":"1.2.6 — Master / export — delivery, not a mastering school","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.6 — Master / export — delivery, not a mastering school","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Bounce a listen file. You are not earning a mastering certificate here.  "},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Goal","Output"],"rows":[["<strong>Master / export</strong>","Level, delivery format","WAV / MP3 for listening or release"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Standard includes <strong>POW-r dithering</strong> on export (KB §2). That is a checkbox, not a career. Your job in this course is: bounce a listen WAV, version the Project, Collect All and Save."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> What file leaves the computer at the end of a deliverable — a Live Set only, or a listen bounce too?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-7","title":"1.2.7 — The injury: mixing while the chorus is unfinished","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.7 — The injury: mixing while the chorus is unfinished","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> If the form is still moving, put the compressor down.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.1, last sentence of the stages note:"},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"You will often loop between Write → Arrange → Record. That is normal. What hurts beginners is mixing while still rewriting the chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"Reddit / EDMProd finishing culture says the same in other words: do not mix during the creative process. Matla’s loop-trap piece: we avoid the hard part (a second section) by doing comfortable work (another plugin). Mixing an unwritten chorus is the same injury with a fancier name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> If you opened a compressor because you were bored of the loop, which job were you actually avoiding?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-2-8","title":"1.2.8 — Which jobs this course owns","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.8 — Which jobs this course owns","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Form, Session, Arrangement, warp/sample, remix, flip, finish. Not synthesis. Not DJ sets.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This course owns:</strong> Write (form), Produce/arrange (contrast), light Record/Edit as they serve form, warp and sample as production, Session song, Arrangement song, remix, flip, finish/export."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This course names but does not degree:</strong> Mix, master."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This course points away:</strong> synthesis (Sound Design), drum science (Drums), two-deck blending (DJ’ing), device encyclopedia (Ableton), full hardware school (Launchkey)."},{"type":"prose","html":"A different list exists in <em>Master Electronic Music Production: 5 Stages of Every Producer</em> (Initiation, Exponential Learning, The Dip, Proficiency, Mastery). That is a <strong>career</strong> curve. Do not replace the six jobs with it. If you are in “The Dip,” the medicine here is still: finish a small sectional piece."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.2 practice:</strong> Take one abandoned idea. Write which of the six stages it died in.   <strong>Section 1.2 checkpoint:</strong> Name the six jobs without saying “make it sound pro.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-1","title":"1.3.1 — What the loop trap feels like","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.1 — What the loop trap feels like","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The same eight bars, another plugin, still no second section.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Symptoms (Matla + local finishing PDFs):"},{"type":"list","items":["You have a kick, some percussion, maybe a bass. The next thought is “???”  ","You tell yourself the idea is not good enough, abandon it, start a new loop, repeat.  ","You tell yourself you are “stuck in the loop,” as if that were a physical law.  ","You open a new instrument because the old one is boring — the section list is still empty."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Cause Matla names: we do what we are comfortable with first (drums, bass), then we hit the work we are not comfortable with (melody, chords, a second section). The trap is avoidance wearing a producer costume."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Which of those symptoms have you done this month?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-2","title":"1.3.2 — A loop is a cell, not a song","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.2 — A loop is a cell, not a song","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Keep the loop. Give it neighbors.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the loop. Give it a <strong>named</strong> neighbor: Verse, Chorus, Build, Breakdown, Intro, Outro — or a club name you will learn in 0.9."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>3 Ways to Write a Drop</em> (local PDF) is blunt: if you can produce a great 8-bar loop you can produce a full song. Failure to develop it is an <strong>arrangement / songwriting</strong> problem, not a “I need more VSTs” problem. Recap of that article: if you are stuck writing a B section, put the VSTs down. Focus on chords and melodies first."},{"type":"prose","html":"That article’s three techniques for a B section (you will reuse these as <em>contrast</em>, not as theory homework):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Iterative</strong> — same chords, new layers / melody / drums / subtract  ","<strong>Development</strong> — same idea, changed (one or two chords, timing, passing chords, pattern, rhythm, voicing)  ","<strong>Diversion</strong> — a new progression that still belongs"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can you keep the loop <em>and</em> admit it is not yet a song?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-3","title":"1.3.3 — Exit 1 — duplicate and subtract","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.3 — Exit 1 — duplicate and subtract","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Copy the clip, take things out, call that the verse.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"This is EDMProd <strong>subtractive arrangement</strong> (cheat sheet strategy 4; article strategy 4) plus <em>3 Ways</em> Tip 7 (remove elements to create contrast) plus the cheat sheet’s single-instrument step “duplicate original clip and simplify… use simplified clip in a different location (i.e., verse).”"},{"type":"prose","html":"How, in producer English (no new synth):"},{"type":"list","items":["Make the loop as complete as you dare (subtractive works better when there is something to remove).  ","Duplicate it.  ","On the copy, <strong>take things out</strong>. Hats. Bass. The lead. Call the thinner copy <strong>Verse</strong> (or <strong>Breakdown</strong>, or <strong>Intro</strong>).  ","The full copy can be <strong>Chorus</strong> or <strong>Drop</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Matla’s wide-brush version on a timeline: paste the loop to a desired length, then remove drums from the intro, remove bass from the breakdown. Start coarse, then make smaller holes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This course’s Session version:</strong> Duplicate the scene. Mute clips. Name both scenes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Did you leave the loop by <em>removing</em>, not by loading a new device?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-4","title":"1.3.4 — Exit 2 — write the next section first (even empty)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.4 — Exit 2 — write the next section first (even empty)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> An empty named scene already beats another hat layer.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Two EDMProd strategies live here:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Linear arrangement</strong> (cheat sheet / article strategy 2): work the intro until it is “enough,” <em>force yourself to move on</em>, work the next section, repeat. Do not keep improving bar 1."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Skeleton arrangement</strong> (strategy 3): mark Intro / Verse / Chorus (or club names) with empty clips or locators <em>before</em> the sounds exist. Optional: a reference track you own, warped, then deleted. Fill the blanks later."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>3 Ways</em> said the key to a smooth arrangement is not snare fills and white noise — it is chords and melodies, and a B section that has a relationship to A. An empty named scene is already a B section. A fifth hat layer is not."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Do you have a scene or locator whose only job is “the next section,” even if it is silent?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-5","title":"1.3.5 — Exit 3 — change density, not the preset","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.5 — Exit 3 — change density, not the preset","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Contrast is how many parts are playing, not a new wavetable.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>3 Ways</em>, Technique 1 (Iterative): same chords in A and B; distinction comes from layers, a new or disguised melody, a new drum loop, more percussion, pads, adding <em>or removing</em> the bass. Technique 2 (Development) changes the idea slightly. Neither technique requires a new synthesizer."},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd strategy 5, “same idea, different instruments”: take the chord clip, change rhythm and sound (pad instead of pluck). That is still density / presentation, and it is allowed — <strong>stock piano or a Core Library pad</strong>, not a Vital detour."},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.2: default sounds are fine."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name two density changes you can make without leaving Analog / a piano preset / a Drum Rack you already have."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-6","title":"1.3.6 — 90 seconds as a finish target","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.6 — 90 seconds as a finish target","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A short finished shape beats an infinite loop.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Reuse 0.1.7 with a stopwatch. Target: <strong>about 90 seconds</strong> of sectional music. KB §9.7’s 2–2.5 minute “first complete song” is a later, larger target. Do not invent a runtime as a genre law."},{"type":"prose","html":"Ninety seconds is long enough to have an intro (or a cold start), a change, and an ending you chose. It is short enough that you cannot hide in a seven-minute additive techno fantasy <em>yet</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Export anything 90 seconds that has two named sections."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-7","title":"1.3.7 — Reference listen: where did their loop become a section?","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.7 — Reference listen: where did their loop become a section?","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On a song you already own, mark the first time the cell changes job.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick a song you <strong>own</strong>. Do not stream-rip. Play it once with a notepad."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write:"},{"type":"list","items":["The first time you hear a repeating cell (often 4 or 8 bars).  ","The first time that cell <strong>changes job</strong> (something enters, something leaves, lyrics return, energy drops). That moment is a <strong>section boundary</strong>.  ","The name you would give the new section (verse, chorus, drop, breakdown — use words you already have; 0.4–0.9 will refine them)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"This is Beat Kitchen’s later homework in miniature: change at phrase boundaries. You are only listening for <em>one</em> boundary today."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One timestamp, one section name, on a record you own."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-3-8","title":"1.3.8 — Write the section list before you add a fifth track","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.8 — Write the section list before you add a fifth track","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Names first. Layers second.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Before another track:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Intro\nVerse\nPre-chorus   (or skip)\nChorus\nBridge       (or skip)\nOutro</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"or a club list (0.9):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">DJ intro\nGroove\nBreakdown\nDrop\nOutro</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Empty names are legal. A fifth track is not illegal — it is just the wrong next move if the list does not exist."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.3 practice:</strong> Take any 8-bar loop. Make a second 8 bars that is <em>less</em>.   <strong>Section 1.3 checkpoint:</strong> You can leave a loop without opening a new instrument."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Part A (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<em>Music-Production-Songwriting-Knowledgebase</em> §1.1–1.2, §5.5, §8.7, §9.4, §9.7  ","OUR GOAL FOR THIS SESSION §3E, §6.7  ","EDMProd, Sam Matla, <em>How to Avoid The 8-Bar Loop Trap</em> (2018/2023) and <em>Loop Trap Cheat Sheet</em> (five workflows)  ","<em>3 Ways to Write a Drop: A Songwriter’s Guide to the Loop Trap</em> (iterative / development / diversion)  ","EDMProd, <em>Master Electronic Music Production: 5 Stages</em> — career curve only; do not replace the six jobs  ","Ableton Live 12 Manual ch. 7.5 (independent Session / Arrangement clips)  ","Beat Kitchen, <em>Arrangement and Energy Management</em> (preview: repetition as feature)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"production-m-1-2","code":"1","title":"Part B: Popular-music form (textbook)","blurb":"Berklee Writing Hit Songs Lesson 1 starts with the chorus. We still define verse first so “chorus” has something to contrast against. Section 1.6 then spends a full unit on chorus / refrain / hook / post-chorus.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-2","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 1 — Part B: Popular-music form (textbook)","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Teach sectional form as a <em>subject</em>, the way a songwriting textbook would, before you fill a Session grid.   <strong>Required.</strong> No gate.   <strong>Do not:</strong> assign Roman-numeral homework as a theory course (point to Piano). Do not force later club labs into ABABCB.   <strong>Listen cards:</strong> Named songs are <strong>optional if you already own them</strong>. Otherwise run the same test on any song you own."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Primary sources:</strong> Wikipedia <em>Song structure</em>, <em>Verse–chorus form</em>, <em>Post-chorus</em>, <em>Thirty-two-bar form</em>; Walter Everett (1999, 2008) as quoted there; Jay Summach, <em>Music Theory Online</em> 17/3 (2011); Andrea Stolpe / Berklee Take Note (bridge) and Berklee Online <em>Writing Hit Songs</em> / <em>Writing and Analyzing Hit Songs</em> syllabi; Asaf Peres, “Everything You Need to Know About the Postchorus” (Top40 Theory, 2018); Jason Blume / songwriting.net; Davidson &amp; Heartwood via Wikipedia; local KB §8.7."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 1.4 — Verse vs chorus","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Define verse vs chorus without saying “the loud part.” State Everett’s tonic claim. Label both on a recording you own."},{"type":"prose","html":"Berklee <em>Writing Hit Songs</em> Lesson 1 starts with the chorus. We still define <strong>verse</strong> first so “chorus” has something to contrast against. Section 1.6 then spends a full unit on chorus / refrain / hook / post-chorus."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-1","title":"1.4.1 — Verse = new words, same (or nearly same) music","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.1 — Verse = new words, same (or nearly same) music","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> When the verse music returns, the lyric almost always changes.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia, following Everett: when the music of the verse returns, it is almost always given a <strong>new set of lyrics</strong>. The music may take slight modifications. The lyric is the thing that <em>must</em> change, or you are not in a new verse — you are repeating."},{"type":"prose","html":"Davidson &amp; Heartwood (via Wikipedia): the verse holds the details — story, events, images, emotions. Each verse has different lyrics from the others. A verse exists primarily to <strong>support</strong> the chorus, musically and lyrically."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the piece is instrumental, treat the lead as the “lyric”: new phrases in verse 2, not a copy-paste of verse 1 with a louder fader (KB §8)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Lyric-sameness test:</strong> Play the first and second verse (on a song you own). Do the words change? If yes, and the music is largely the same, you found verses."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Define verse without the word “quiet.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-2","title":"1.4.2 — Chorus = same words, same music, higher intensity","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.2 — Chorus = same words, same music, higher intensity","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The chorus keeps its lyric and is the higher-intensity section.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia / Everett: the chorus usually <strong>retains the same lyrics</strong> every time its music appears. It is always of greater musical and emotional intensity than the verse. Everett (1999, via Wikipedia): the chorus “gets its name from a usual thickening of texture from the addition of backing vocals,” is a discrete section, “nearly always prolongs the tonic and carries an unvaried poetic text.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"The chorus carries the <strong>main idea</strong>. The title often lives here (KB §8.7; Berklee hit-song teaching)."},{"type":"prose","html":"A chorus that is only louder, with the same lyric density and the same register, is a failed chorus <em>as form</em> even if the fader went up. Intensity ≠ volume. Preview of KB §9.4 (full table in Module 2): change <strong>at least two</strong> of density, register, hats, width, harmony, fill, bass rhythm."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Define chorus without the word “loud.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-3","title":"1.4.3 — Everett: verse prolongs the tonic","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.3 — Everett: verse prolongs the tonic","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The verse is a unit that stays “home” so the chorus can still be home, but bigger.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Everett (via Wikipedia): “the verse is to be understood as a unit that <strong>prolongs the tonic</strong>.” The home chord of the song can be prolonged with closely related chords (in C major, iii = E minor and vi = A minor share tones with C). This is <strong>literacy</strong>, not a figured-bass exam. Do not write Roman-numeral homework. If you want that school, it is Piano."},{"type":"prose","html":"What a producer needs: verse harmony often <em>wanders less</em> than people think. It sits near home so the chorus can still be home — thicker, higher, more remembered."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> “Prolong the tonic” in one plain sentence."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-4","title":"1.4.4 — The chorus is not “the loud part”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.4 — The chorus is not “the loud part”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Contrast checklist preview — at least two levers, not one fader.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §8.7: “Contrast is arrangement + harmony + density, not only ‘louder.’”"},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.4 (learn the list now; use it for real in Module 2):"},{"type":"prose","html":"Make a chorus feel like a chorus by changing <strong>at least two</strong> of:"},{"type":"list","items":["More / fewer instruments  ","Higher melodic range  ","Brighter EQ / open hats  ","Wider stereo (carefully)  ","Harmonic lift (e.g. add IV or a secondary dominant later)  ","Drum fill into the downbeat of the chorus  ","Bass rhythm denser or more locked  "],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name two levers that are not volume."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-5","title":"1.4.5 — Title placement — often the chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.5 — Title placement — often the chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The remembered name of the song usually sits in the chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §8.7: title often lives in the chorus. Berklee <em>Writing Hit Songs</em> Lesson 1 treats title placement as a chorus craft. This is a <strong>tendency</strong>, not a law. Some songs title from a refrain line in the verse. Label honestly."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> On a song you own, is the title in the chorus, a refrain, or somewhere else?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-6","title":"1.4.6 — Listen: mark verse vs chorus on one radio song","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.6 — Listen: mark verse vs chorus on one radio song","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On a song you already own, mark V and Ch.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Optional owned-catalog listen if you have it: Berklee Analyzing Hit Songs names <em>Blinding Lights</em> (The Weeknd) and <em>Somebody That I Used to Know</em> (Gotye) as form case studies. Use them <strong>only if you already own them</strong>. Otherwise any radio-shaped song you own."},{"type":"prose","html":"On paper, left to right:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">0:00  ________\n0:15  ________\n...</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Write <strong>V</strong> or <strong>Ch</strong> (or “?”). Use the lyric-sameness test, not the loudness test."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One page of marks. No timestamps invented as laws."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-7","title":"1.4.7 — Listen: a song that starts on the chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.7 — Listen: a song that starts on the chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Name the exception so the default is not a religion.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia notes exceptions: some songs start on the chorus (<em>She Loves You</em> is the named early rock example). Davidson &amp; Heartwood (via Wikipedia) also list chorus-first shapes (Pete Seeger <em>Turn! Turn! Turn!</em>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you own a chorus-first song, mark it. If you do not, write: “I do not own a chorus-first example yet” and skip. Do not rip."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The default is verse-then-chorus. The exception has a name."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-4-8","title":"1.4.8 — Write two 8-bar clips that could be verse vs chorus without a new synth","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.4.8 — Write two 8-bar clips that could be verse vs chorus without a new synth","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Density and lyric-sameness, not a new patch.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"In Live, two clips (or two scenes), <strong>same instrument</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Clip A: sparser. Imagine new words each time.  ","Clip B: thicker (more notes, higher register, or an extra doubled line). Imagine the same words each time."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not load Vital. Core Library piano or a stock synth is enough."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.4 practice:</strong> Timeline on paper; mark V and Ch on one song you like.   <strong>Section 1.4 checkpoint:</strong> Define verse vs chorus without “loud.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-1","title":"1.5.1 — Four names, one job","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.1 — Four names, one job","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Pre-chorus, build, channel, transitional bridge — same corridor.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: an optional section after the verse, also called <strong>build</strong>, <strong>channel</strong>, or <strong>transitional bridge</strong>. It connects verse to chorus with intermediary material."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Not the same word as “bridge.”</strong> A bridge (0.7) typically arrives after chorus 2, once. A transitional bridge <em>is</em> a pre-chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"Jay Summach (<em>MTO</em> 17/3, 2011): by the mid-1980s the pre-chorus had become a standard feature inside verse–chorus form. In <em>Talking in Your Sleep</em> (Romantics), intensity at the end of the verse does <strong>not</strong> dump into the chorus — a pre-chorus gains more momentum first (busier bass, quicker lyric phrasing, shorter formal units). His larger claim: a pre-chorus turns a two-section song into a <strong>three-section, more teleological</strong> form. Momentum-building is no longer a two-bar transition at the end of the verse; it is the <strong>job of a whole section</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musicians also say <strong>climb</strong> or <strong>ramp</strong> for a short, not-quite-independent pre-chorus (Summach §22, Blondie <em>Call Me</em>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Say “channel” and mean a section, not a mixer strip."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-2","title":"1.5.2 — Why the verse sometimes cannot go straight to the chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.2 — Why the verse sometimes cannot go straight to the chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> If the verse already arrives, you may not need a ramp.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Summach §2: devices that build momentum include groove, lyric phrasing, length of units, dynamics, register, instrumentation, timbre, progression, harmonic rhythm. By some combination, the pre-chorus <em>transforms</em> verse–chorus into a three-part climb."},{"type":"prose","html":"Andrea Stolpe (Berklee pre-chorus video): ask what is happening at the <strong>end of the verse</strong> that makes a pre-chorus necessary. If the verse already arrives, you may not want one."},{"type":"prose","html":"Summach’s Billboard study (700 Annual Top 20 songs, 1955–89): verse–chorus songs without pre-choruses are not “deficient.” Funk, some singer-songwriter, and some disco <em>prefer</em> a flatter energy. Taste fluctuates. Do not force a pre-chorus into every lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> When would you skip a pre-chorus on purpose?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-3","title":"1.5.3 — New harmony as the ramp","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.3 — New harmony as the ramp","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> IV, ii, or a pictured secondary dominant — literacy, not homework.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: the pre-chorus typically uses <strong>subdominant</strong> (IV or ii) or similar transitional harmonies. Everett: a two-phrase verse with basic chords is often followed by a “harmonically probing” passage into the chorus. If verse and chorus share a loop, the pre-chorus is often the <strong>only new progression</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pictured example (Wikipedia, key of C, chorus that briefly treats G as home): a bar of A minor and a bar of D7 as ii–V of G. There is no D7 in C major; a listener hears it as a <strong>secondary dominant</strong>. Teach as <strong>one picture</strong>. Do not assign a worksheet."},{"type":"prose","html":"Summach adds: many pre-choruses inherit “departure” features of the sentence (<strong>srdc</strong> — statement, restatement, departure, conclusion): faster harmonic rhythm, fragmented lyrics, emphasis on subdominant after a tonic-heavy verse."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Why might a pre-chorus use chords the verse never used?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-4","title":"1.5.4 — Stolpe: contrast the motifs, keep it simple","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.4 — Stolpe: contrast the motifs, keep it simple","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Melodic contrast in the ramp; do not write a third song.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Stolpe (Berklee): melodically, make sure you have contrast. A simple verse motif pattern (motif 1, motif 2, motif 1, motif 2) is enough. The pre-chorus should feel like a <em>new</em> small idea, not a second chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"Producer translation: fewer elements → more elements is legal (next lesson). A brand-new 16-bar hook in the pre-chorus is usually too much song."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Is your pre-chorus smaller than your chorus?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-5","title":"1.5.5 — When not to write a pre-chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.5 — When not to write a pre-chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Two-section songs are complete songs.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Skip a pre-chorus when:"},{"type":"list","items":["The verse already climbs (Stolpe).  ","The genre’s clock is additive 8/16 (house/techno — 0.9), not verse–pre–chorus.  ","Summach’s funk / flat-energy case: a pre-chorus would fight the groove’s stasis.  ","You cannot summarize what the verse has been doing, so you cannot control the contrast (Stolpe’s later bridge advice applies here too)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name one good reason to have only verse and chorus."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-6","title":"1.5.6 — Pre-chorus as fewer-elements → more-elements","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.6 — Pre-chorus as fewer-elements → more-elements","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Production ramp: add, do not redesign.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Session picture: Verse scene is sparse. Pre-chorus scene adds hats, or a second line, or a fill lane. Chorus scene is the full band. No new synth required."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is Summach’s “momentum as the function of an entire section,” done with mute and duplicate."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can you launch verse → pre → chorus by adding clips, not replacing the song?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-7","title":"1.5.7 — Listen: find a pre-chorus that is not just “hats get busier”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.7 — Listen: find a pre-chorus that is not just “hats get busier”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On a song you own, find a real ramp.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Listen for: new chords, quicker lyric, higher register, a fill that is <em>not</em> yet the chorus. If you only hear busier hats, write “hats only — maybe a climb, maybe not.” Honesty over fake labels."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One owned song, one marked ramp or an honest “none.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-5-8","title":"1.5.8 — Empty pre-chorus scene: 4 or 8 bars that only exist to climb","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.5.8 — Empty pre-chorus scene: 4 or 8 bars that only exist to climb","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Reserve the corridor even if it is quiet.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Make a scene named <code>Pre</code>. Four or eight bars. It may contain almost nothing. Its job is to exist so the chorus does not crash into the verse."},{"type":"prose","html":"Summach: a two-bar swell at the end of a verse is a <strong>transition</strong>. A pre-chorus is a <strong>section</strong>. Give it enough time to be a section, or do not call it one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.5 checkpoint:</strong> “Channel” means a section."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-1","title":"1.6.1 — Chorus as section vs refrain as line","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.1 — Chorus as section vs refrain as line","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A refrain is too short to be a section.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"The words are often used interchangeably. When a distinction is made (Cope; Davidson &amp; Heartwood, via Wikipedia):"},{"type":"list","items":["A <strong>chorus</strong> is a <em>section</em> that repeats musically and lyrically and holds the main idea.  ","A <strong>refrain</strong> is a short repeated line that is <strong>not long enough to be a section</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia examples of refrain: “yeah, yeah, yeah” (<em>She Loves You</em>); “fa la la la la” (<em>Deck the Halls</em>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Summach is stricter and useful: a lyric-invariant passage is a <strong>refrain</strong> (part of the strophe) when it is too short or when hearing it as a new section would split a pattern still in progress. It is a <strong>chorus</strong> (its own section) when it is long enough (often eight bars or more) <em>and</em> it completes its own small pattern. Roger Miller <em>King of the Road</em>: four-bar tail refrain, not a chorus. Everly Brothers <em>Cathy’s Clown</em>: separate verse and chorus sections."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Point at a repeated line in a song you own. Section, or line?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-2","title":"1.6.2 — Hook is a remembered idea, not a section name","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.2 — Hook is a remembered idea, not a section name","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The hook may live in the chorus, a riff, or a post-chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>hook</strong> is the remembered musical or lyric idea (Watson; Songstuff, via Wikipedia). It often lives in the chorus. It can also be a guitar riff, a vocal chop, or a post-chorus chant."},{"type":"prose","html":"Hook ≠ chorus. A chorus <em>contains</em> a hook more often than not. A hook can exist without a chorus (strophic songs with a refrain line)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name a hook that is not “the whole chorus.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-3","title":"1.6.3 — Why “chorus” historically means thicker texture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.3 — Why “chorus” historically means thicker texture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Backing voices — the reason for the name.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Everett again: the name comes from a usual <strong>thickening of texture</strong> from backing vocals. Producer translation: if your “chorus” is one mono lead and nothing else changed, you have not yet made a chorus <em>as a section</em>, even if you named the scene <code>Chorus</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> What did you <em>add</em> when the chorus arrives?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-4","title":"1.6.4 — Post-chorus: Peres’s two conditions","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.4 — Post-chorus: Peres’s two conditions","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Energy stays up, and there is a hook — or it is not a post-chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Asaf Peres (Top40 Theory, 2018; Wikipedia <em>Post-chorus</em>):"},{"type":"prose","html":"A post-chorus (or postchorus) comes immediately after the chorus. Two conditions:"},{"type":"list","items":["It <strong>maintains or increases</strong> sonic energy — otherwise it is a bridge or a verse.  ","It contains a <strong>melodic hook</strong> (vocal or instrumental) — otherwise it is a transition."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Berklee <em>Writing and Analyzing Hit Songs</em> Lesson 6 lists pre- and post-chorus as “other sections.” Lesson 8 returns to refining chorus and post-chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Recite the two conditions."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-5","title":"1.6.5 — Attached vs detached vs hybrid","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.5 — Attached vs detached vs hybrid","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Optional owned-catalog listens for the three types.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Peres’s types (Wikipedia summary):"},{"type":"table","headers":["Type","What it does","Optional owned example"],"rows":[["<strong>Detached</strong>","New melody / lyrics after the chorus","Sia, <em>Chandelier</em> — chorus “swing from the chandelier”; post-chorus “holding on…”"],["<strong>Attached</strong>","Repeats the hook with vocables / little new content","Rihanna, <em>Umbrella</em> — “ella, ella”; Ed Sheeran <em>Shape of You</em>; Maroon 5 <em>Girls Like You</em>"],["<strong>Hybrid</strong>","Keeps the hook (attached) <em>and</em> adds new content (detached)","Peres’s hybrid category"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Use those titles <strong>only if you own the records</strong>. Otherwise find any chorus that either (a) starts a new hook after it, or (b) chants the last line with “oh / yeah.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One owned song, one type or “none.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-6","title":"1.6.6 — If energy drops after the chorus, it is not a post-chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.6 — If energy drops after the chorus, it is not a post-chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A drop in energy is verse 2 or a bridge.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Peres: if sonic energy drops, you are probably in the second verse or the bridge. If there is no melodic hook, it is a transition."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not label a quiet guitar break “post-chorus” because it is after the chorus. Sequence is not the test. Energy + hook is the test."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Apply the test to 8 bars after a chorus you own."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-7","title":"1.6.7 — You are not required to write one","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.7 — You are not required to write one","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Know the name. Do not force the section.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Post-chorus is increasingly common in modern hits (Blume, BMI; Wikipedia). It is still <strong>optional</strong>. This course requires you to <em>hear</em> it, not to write one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can you leave a song as ABABCB with no post-chorus and still be correct?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-6-8","title":"1.6.8 — Label one commercial song","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.6.8 — Label one commercial song","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Chorus / refrain / post-chorus / none — on a song you own.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"One listen card. Four possible marks. Honesty over forcing Peres’s vocabulary onto a folk song."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.6 checkpoint:</strong> You do not call every extra 4 bars after the chorus a bridge."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-1","title":"1.7.1 — Bridge after chorus 2 — once","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.1 — Bridge after chorus 2 — once","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Contrast, then the last chorus. Not a pre-chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Everett (via Wikipedia): a bridge contrasts the verse, usually ends on the dominant, often a strong re-transition. Davidson &amp; Heartwood: it breaks the repetitive pattern; <strong>words and music</strong> change."},{"type":"prose","html":"Typical placement: after the <strong>second chorus</strong>, once. Then the last chorus (often bigger)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Not a pre-chorus. Not a post-chorus. If energy stays high and a hook continues, you are still in Peres territory, not a bridge."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Where does a bridge usually sit in ABABCB?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-2","title":"1.7.2 — Stolpe: contrast, character, clarity","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.2 — Stolpe: contrast, character, clarity","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> You cannot control contrast if you cannot summarize the song so far.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Andrea Stolpe, Berklee Take Note, “Writing Bridges for Your Songs”:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Musical contrast</strong> — you must be able to summarize what the music has been doing, or you cannot control what the bridge contrasts.  ","<strong>Lyrical contrast</strong> — it does not have to “say something different” as a slogan; it has to take the listener somewhere.  ","<strong>Character and clarity</strong> — a fresh angle before the final chorus."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Producer translation: thinner texture, new chords, a key lift, or a solo-like lead — then return. KB §8.7: contrast by new chords, thin texture, key lift, etc."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Summarize your song so far in one sentence. That sentence is what the bridge pushes against."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-3","title":"1.7.3 — Middle eight as a common bridge type","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.3 — Middle eight as a common bridge type","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Often 8 bars. Do not require 8 bars.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: “middle eight” is a common bridge type — often eight bars, new melody and lyrics. Do not require eight. A 4-bar bridge that does the job is a bridge. An 16-bar ramble is not better because it is longer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Middle eight is a <em>type</em>, not a law."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-4","title":"1.7.4 — Intro jobs: key, groove, atmosphere, cadence","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.4 — Intro jobs: key, groove, atmosphere, cadence","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Usually instrumental; ends so a singer (or lead) can enter.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia <em>Introduction</em>: usually music, no words; builds suspense into the first downbeat; sets atmosphere; often gives the singer the key; commonly ends on <strong>tonic or dominant</strong>. The rhythm section typically plays in the feel of the song that follows."},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §8.7: establish groove / hook fragment."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name the four jobs (key, groove, atmosphere, cadence)."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-5","title":"1.7.5 — Risky intro vs “use a piece of the song”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.5 — Risky intro vs “use a piece of the song”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Least risky: preview the song. Riskier: material that does not.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: the least risky intro uses material from the song (themes, chords, beat). The alternative uses material that does <strong>not</strong> preview the song, to create uncertainty."},{"type":"prose","html":"Both are legal. Club-form intros (0.9) are a third species: long DJ corridors. Do not call a 32-bar DJ intro a pop intro."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Which kind of intro did the last song you owned use?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-6","title":"1.7.6 — Outro / coda / tag / fade / ritardando","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.6 — Outro / coda / tag / fade / ritardando","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Signal the end so the last bar is not a power cut.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: if a song ends on the last bar of a chorus with no outro, it can feel too abrupt. Outro tools: <strong>fade</strong>, <strong>ritardando</strong> (gradual slow-down), <strong>vamp/tag</strong>, repeated chorus fragment. Generally do not introduce new melodies. DJs also read the outro as a mix-out — that skill belongs to the DJ course."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Name two ways to end that are not “stop on beat 1.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-7","title":"1.7.7 — Elision and ad lib — names only","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.7 — Elision and ad lib — names only","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Overlap so cadences do not stop the train; free vocal at the end.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Elision</strong> (Wikipedia): sections overlap so a cadence does not feel like a full stop. Literacy only."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Ad lib</strong> (Wikipedia): usually in the coda, the lead breaks the established lyric/melody. Distinct from “ad lib” as a general term for any free take."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can define both without having to write either."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-7-8","title":"1.7.8 — Listen: annotate intro, bridge, outro on one song","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.7.8 — Listen: annotate intro, bridge, outro on one song","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On a song you own, mark the edges.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"One owned song. Three marks. If there is no bridge, write “none.” Country Roads is Wikipedia’s “has a bridge” example; Stevie Wonder <em>You Are the Sunshine of My Life</em> is their “has none” example — use only if you own them."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.7 checkpoint:</strong> Bridge ≠ pre-chorus. You can say why."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-1","title":"1.8.1 — The Wikipedia modern-pop string","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.1 — The Wikipedia modern-pop string","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Intro – verse – pre – chorus – verse – pre – chorus – bridge – chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia <em>Song structure</em>:"},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"The most common format in modern popular music is introduction (intro), verse, pre-chorus, chorus, verse, pre-chorus, chorus, bridge, and chorus, with an optional outro."},{"type":"prose","html":"Verse, chorus, and pre-chorus usually <strong>repeat</strong>. Intro, bridge, and coda are usually used <strong>once</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Write the string from memory."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-2","title":"1.8.2 — ABAB and ABABCB in words and letters","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.2 — ABAB and ABABCB in words and letters","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Letters are sections. Say them in English too.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Jason Blume / songwriting.net: common hit forms since the Beatles era include verse–chorus–verse–chorus and <strong>verse–chorus–verse–chorus–bridge–chorus</strong> (ABABCB)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Watson examples of ABABCB (via Wikipedia): Mellencamp <em>Hurts So Good</em>; Tina Turner <em>What’s Love Got to Do with It</em>; ZZ Top <em>Sharp Dressed Man</em> — optional if owned."},{"type":"table","headers":["Letters","Words"],"rows":[["A","Verse"],["B","Chorus"],["C","Bridge"],["ABAB","Verse chorus verse chorus"],["ABABCB","…plus bridge and last chorus"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Say ABABCB in words."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-3","title":"1.8.3 — Variations: ABABCAB, double last chorus, chorus-first","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.3 — Variations: ABABCAB, double last chorus, chorus-first","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The default bends. Label what you actually hear.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia “Variation on the basic structure”: ABABCAB (Pretenders <em>Back on the Chain Gang</em>), ABABCBAB (Poison <em>Every Rose Has Its Thorn</em>), chorus-first ABA, and so on. A doubled last chorus or a tag is a variation, not a new religion."},{"type":"prose","html":"Berklee Analyzing Hit Songs Lesson 6: unusual forms exist; create <strong>clear sections</strong> and label them honestly."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> If your song is “weird,” can you still name the sections without forcing ABABCB?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-4","title":"1.8.4 — Strophic / AAA — name and one listen","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.4 — Strophic / AAA — name and one listen","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Same music, new words, no separate chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Strophic form: each stanza gets the same music. Folk / hymn cousin. Wikipedia named examples include Dylan <em>The Times They Are a-Changin’</em>, <em>Amazing Grace</em>, <em>House of the Rising Sun</em> — use only if owned. Summach: the strophe is the one section type; a short invariant line inside it is a <strong>refrain</strong>, not a chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"Name it. Do not write a 12-verse ballad unless you want to."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> How is AAA different from ABAB?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-5","title":"1.8.5 — 32-bar AABA — name, do not write a standard","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.5 — 32-bar AABA — name, do not write a standard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> 8+8+8+8. B is the bridge.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia <em>Thirty-two-bar form</em>: four sections, usually eight bars each. Two A’s, a contrasting B (bridge / middle eight), last A. Jazz-standard cousin (<em>I Got Rhythm</em>). Pop cousin (<em>Yesterday</em>). Summach also calls this the Tin Pan Alley song."},{"type":"prose","html":"Name it. Do not require writing one. The B section is a <strong>bridge</strong>, not a pre-chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> How many bars in the textbook AABA, if each letter is 8?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-6","title":"1.8.6 — 12-bar blues as a form option","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.6 — 12-bar blues as a form option","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Named for a later skippable lab. Not a theory course.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: 12-bar blues is a named form. Summach uses <em>Hound Dog</em> as a familiar <strong>aa′b</strong> (sentence-like) 12-bar. This course does not teach blues harmony. A later skippable lab may use 12-bar as a <em>grid</em>. Until then: name only."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> 12-bar is a form option, not this week’s homework."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-7","title":"1.8.7 — Compound AABA — pop as nested form","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.7 — Compound AABA — pop as nested form","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Each A can already be verse–chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Wikipedia: verse–chorus (or ABA) may combine with AABA into <strong>compound AABA</strong> — every A or B can itself contain more than one section (for example verse–chorus). Modern pop can be read that way: the “B” of the large form is the bridge."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can ABABCB be <em>one</em> big AABA in disguise?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-8","title":"1.8.8 — Unusual forms — if it works, label it honestly","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.8 — Unusual forms — if it works, label it honestly","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Clear sections beat a forced template.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Berklee Analyzing Hit Songs Lesson 6: hit-song form standards, unusual forms, creating clear sections. Optional owned case studies: <em>Blinding Lights</em>; <em>Somebody That I Used to Know</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a song has no chorus, say so. If it is through-composed (little repetition), say so. Do not invent a pre-chorus to look clever."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Write “unusual” on a song you own only if the default string fails."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-9","title":"1.8.9 — Listen lab: annotate one radio song with a ruler","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.9 — Listen lab: annotate one radio song with a ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On a song you own, mark the Wikipedia string or the honest variant.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"One owned song. A ruler on paper. Letters or names. This is the section’s main homework."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can point at the page and say the section names aloud."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-8-10","title":"1.8.10 — Write your default map on paper","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.8.10 — Write your default map on paper","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> You may change it later. You may not stay blank.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Write <em>your</em> default:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Intro\nVerse\nPre-chorus   (yes / no)\nChorus\nVerse 2\nPre\nChorus\nBridge       (yes / no)\nChorus\nOutro</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"You will replace this in club labs. You need a default so Session scenes have names tomorrow."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.8 practice:</strong> Timeline; mark A/B/C on one song.   <strong>Section 1.8 checkpoint:</strong> Recite ABABCB and the Wikipedia string."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Part B (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Wikipedia <em>Song structure</em>; <em>Verse–chorus form</em>; <em>Post-chorus</em>; <em>Thirty-two-bar form</em>  ","Walter Everett, <em>The Beatles as Musicians</em> (1999); <em>The Foundations of Rock</em> (2008)  ","Jay Summach, “The Structure, Function, and Genesis of the Prechorus,” <em>Music Theory Online</em> 17/3 (2011)  ","Andrea Stolpe, Berklee Take Note (bridge); Berklee Online <em>Writing Hit Songs</em> and <em>Writing and Analyzing Hit Songs</em> syllabi  ","Asaf Peres, “Everything You Need to Know About the Postchorus,” Top40 Theory (2018)  ","Jason Blume / songwriting.net; Davidson &amp; Heartwood via Wikipedia  ","Local KB §8.7, §9.4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"production-m-1-3","code":"1","title":"Part C: Club form, energy, project hygiene, first MK4 touch","blurb":"Beat Kitchen ch. 6, taught here as literacy and reused in every club lab and in Module 8:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-3","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 1 — Part C: Club form, energy, project hygiene, first MK4 touch","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Contrast the DJ clock with the pop clock. Learn the 8-bar phrase as electronic structure. Then make the computer a place you can find tomorrow, and touch the Launchkey as a production surface.   <strong>Required.</strong> No gate.   <strong>Do not:</strong> teach kick/snare science (Drums). Do not invent BPMs. If Beat Kitchen and a local cheat sheet disagree, <strong>local wins</strong> or both are shown.   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Beat Kitchen <em>The Genre Landscape</em> + <em>Arrangement and Energy Management</em> (CC BY-NC-SA); <em>House Music Cheat Sheet</em>; <em>DNB Cheat Sheet</em>; local dubstep KB form pages; KB §8.7 energy map; <em>The Advanced Guide to Tension and Energy</em>; <em>The Ultimate Guide to Build-ups</em>; Live 12 Manual ch. 3, 5, 6, 7; Launchkey / KB §5."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 1.9 — Club form and the 8-bar rule","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Pop clock vs DJ clock. 8-bar phrase as unit. Why house intros are long and 140/trap drops are pop-shaped. Draw one energy curve."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen ch. 6, taught here as literacy and reused in every club lab and in Module 8:"},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"Electronic arrangement is not verse-chorus-verse. It is energy management — addition, subtraction, and transformation. The 8-bar phrase is the structural unit."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-1","title":"1.9.1 — Pop clock vs DJ clock","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.1 — Pop clock vs DJ clock","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Radio songs tell a lyric story. Club tracks serve a floor and a mix.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Two clocks, both legal. This course will not pick one for every lab."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Pop clock (0.4–0.8)","DJ / club clock"],"rows":[["Unit of meaning","Verse, chorus, bridge (lyric + hook)","8- and 16-bar cells; energy state"],["Intro","Often short; sets key/groove","Often <strong>16–32+ bars</strong> so a DJ can mix"],["Contrast","New words / thicker hook","Add or subtract a layer"],["Length (Beat Kitchen)","Often 3–5 min in drop genres","House/techno often <strong>5–8 min</strong>"],["Drop","May not exist","House: full groove <strong>returns</strong>. 140/trap: <strong>texture</strong> shift"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Calling a 32-bar DJ intro a “verse” is a vocabulary error. There is usually no new lyric story. It is a <strong>mix-in corridor</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You do not call a 32-bar DJ intro a verse."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-2","title":"1.9.2 — The 8-bar phrase","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.2 — The 8-bar phrase","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Electronic music’s default cell. Deviating should be a decision.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen ch. 6: just as pop often thinks in 4-bar phrases, electronic production builds in <strong>8-bar blocks</strong>. Elements enter, exit, or transform at 8-bar (sometimes 16-bar) boundaries. Listeners subconsciously expect changes there."},{"type":"prose","html":"Eight bars is long enough to establish a pattern and short enough to keep a listener. A 4-bar transition is legal. A 16-bar breakdown is legal. <strong>Deviating from 8 should be a decision</strong>, not an accident."},{"type":"prose","html":"Count: the kick enters at bar 1; a hat at bar 9; a bass at bar 17 — Beat Kitchen’s pictured habit. The numbers change by genre; the principle does not."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Tap only on bar 1 of each 8-bar cell for one owned club track."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-3","title":"1.9.3 — Additive form — add/subtract layers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.3 — Additive form — add/subtract layers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Each 8 or 16 bars, one thing enters or leaves.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen ch. 1, house and techno: builds happen through <strong>addition</strong>. Each 8 or 16 bars introduces or removes an element. The “drop” is usually a return to the full groove after a breakdown, not a massive textural shift."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the same family as EDMProd subtractive arrangement (0.3) and KB mute-as-contrast. Club form makes it the <em>entire</em> architecture, not a verse/chorus costume."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Write an add/subtract list for 64 bars without naming a chorus."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-4","title":"1.9.4 — Drop as texture change vs “full groove returns”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.4 — Drop as texture change vs “full groove returns”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> House drop ≠ 140 drop. Use the right verb.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>House / techno drop:</strong> usually the <strong>full groove returns</strong> after a breakdown. Not a new planet.  ","<strong>Dubstep / trap drop:</strong> a <strong>dramatic shift in texture and energy</strong>. The arrangement is pop-shaped (intro–build–drop–breakdown–second build–second drop–outro). Tracks often 3–5 minutes."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Local dubstep KB: drop is a texture/energy shift; snare-on-3 is a <strong>section feel</strong>, not a drum exam."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you say “drop” in a house lab, you mean “the band is back.” If you say “drop” in a 140 lab, you mean “the world changed.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One sentence each for the two drop types."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-5","title":"1.9.5 — Breakdown is a reset, not a sad verse","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.5 — Breakdown is a reset, not a sad verse","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Strip so the ear can hear the next arrival.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen ch. 6: a breakdown is the opposite of a drop. Remove kick, bass, most rhythm. What remains is usually a pad, a vocal, a fragment. After dense minutes, 16 bars of almost-nothing lets the ear recalibrate. When parts return, each one registers."},{"type":"prose","html":"A breakdown is not a “sad verse.” There may be no lyric. The job is <strong>reset</strong>, not storytelling."},{"type":"prose","html":"How much you strip depends on genre: Beat Kitchen — a tech-house breakdown might keep a pulse; a trance breakdown might go to a pad and a vocal."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> What does the listener need to feel when energy returns?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-6","title":"1.9.6 — Build / riser as a section type","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.6 — Build / riser as a section type","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Tension before payoff — a section, not only a white-noise sample.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen: a <strong>build</strong> tells the listener something is coming. Tools: rising pitch (sweeps, pitch-automated lines), denser rhythm (snare rolls that get faster), opening a filter, volume swells. Most builds use several at once."},{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>riser</strong> is an upward-sweeping sound (filtered noise, ascending synth, reverse crash). This course treats risers as <strong>arrangement objects</strong> (clips you launch or automate). Designing the synth is Sound Design."},{"type":"prose","html":"Vary them. Beat Kitchen: a short 4-bar build into a verse-level section; a longer 16-bar build into a main drop; a <strong>fake</strong> build that becomes a breakdown. Predictable payoffs stop earning attention."},{"type":"prose","html":"Local companion: <em>The Ultimate Guide to Build-ups</em> (form/automation, not a new wavetable)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> A build is a section type. A riser is one tool inside it."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-7","title":"1.9.7 — Energy curves — draw before you arrange","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.7 — Energy curves — draw before you arrange","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The zoomed-out shape of intensity. Flat is a problem.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen: zoom out. The track should have an <strong>energy curve</strong> — where intensity rises, peaks, drops, resolves."},{"type":"prose","html":"One pictured electronic shape:"},{"type":"prose","html":"Intro (low) → Build 1 → Drop 1 (peak) → Breakdown (minimum) → Build 2 (often longer) → Drop 2 (peak, <strong>must differ</strong>) → Outro"},{"type":"prose","html":"Other shapes exist. Progressive house may rise for minutes. Techno may <strong>plateau</strong> with subtle shifts. Dubstep may have several short-build drops."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you plot the track and it is a flat line, the listener has nowhere to go. If it is random spikes, there is no arc. <strong>Draw the curve on paper before you arrange.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One sketched curve. High points labeled."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-8","title":"1.9.8 — KB pop energy percentages as one pictured map","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.8 — KB pop energy percentages as one pictured map","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A pop picture, not physics.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Local KB §8.7 energy map (<strong>one pictured curve</strong>, not a law):"},{"type":"table","headers":["Section","Pictured intensity"],"rows":[["Intro","50%"],["Verse","55%"],["Pre","70%"],["Chorus","90%"],["Verse 2","60%"],["Chorus","95%"],["Bridge","40–70%"],["Final chorus","100%"],["Outro","down"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it to <em>see</em> a pop shape. Do not automate every fader to these numbers. Club tracks will not match this table — that is the point of 0.9.1."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Why would a techno plateau refuse this table?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-9","title":"1.9.9 — Why this course will not pick one clock for every lab","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.9 — Why this course will not pick one clock for every lab","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Genre is a starting coordinate.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Beat Kitchen ch. 1: electronic music is not one genre. Tempo, drum pattern, and arrangement structure are the first decisions. Sitting down to “make electronic music” with no target often produces a house tempo + dubstep bass + trap hats."},{"type":"prose","html":"This course therefore:"},{"type":"list","items":["Teaches <strong>pop form</strong> as the always-on default (0.4–0.8).  ","Teaches <strong>club energy</strong> as a second literacy (this section).  ","Offers <strong>skippable labs</strong> so you can inhabit one clock at a time.  ","Lets capstones use a lab you opened, or pop."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> What goes wrong if you mix clocks by accident?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-9-10","title":"1.9.10 — Two listen cards: one pop, one club","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.9.10 — Two listen cards: one pop, one club","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Label both honestly, on music you own.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Two owned tracks."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pop clock:</strong> mark intro / verse / pre / chorus / bridge / outro (or the honest variant).  ","<strong>Club clock:</strong> mark 8-bar cells; note whether the drop is “groove returns” or “texture shift.”"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not force the pop string onto a techno record."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Cited tempo bands (show, do not average):</strong>"},{"type":"table","headers":["Genre","Local or named web","Notes"],"rows":[["House","Cheat sheet ~120–130, usually 125–128; Beat Kitchen 118–130, center 124","Show both"],["Techno","Beat Kitchen 120–140, center 128–135; confirm <em>How To Make Techno</em> or gap","—"],["Dubstep","Local ~132–142 / template 140; Beat Kitchen 135–145, center 140; Berklee Now <strong>140 / 4/4</strong>","140 is not physics"],["DnB","<em>DNB Cheat Sheet</em> 170–180 / beginner 174–175; Beat Kitchen 160–180, center 174","—"],["Electronic trap","<em>How To Make Trap</em> 140–160; Beat Kitchen 130–170 half-time","Show both"],["Lo-fi","Beat Kitchen 70–90, center 80","—"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.9 practice:</strong> Two listen cards + one sketched energy curve.   <strong>Section 1.9 checkpoint:</strong> You do not call a 32-bar DJ intro a verse."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-1","title":"1.10.1 — Set vs Project folder","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.1 — Set vs Project folder","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The .als is not the song. The Project folder is where the audio lives.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Live Set</strong> (<code>.als</code>) is the document. A <strong>Project</strong> is the folder Live creates around it, which can hold Samples, Ableton Project Info, and later collected files (Live Manual ch. 5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you only email the <code>.als</code>, the other computer will look for samples that are not there. The Project folder is the unit you back up."},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.2: save versions <code>SongName_v01</code>, <code>v02</code>. Never overwrite the only good take."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> On disk, can you point at the folder, not only the <code>.als</code>?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-2","title":"1.10.2 — Save vs Collect All and Save","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.2 — Save vs Collect All and Save","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Collect All copies samples into the Project so the Set still opens next year.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Save Live Set</strong> writes the current Set (File menu; Manual 5). It does <strong>not</strong> by itself copy every sample you dragged from elsewhere."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Collect All and Save</strong> (File menu) is the shortcut that <strong>collects external files into the Project folder</strong> and then saves (Manual 5.8 / File Manager “Collect and Save”). Confirm the File Manager if Live asks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it before you zip a Project, move drives, or call a deliverable done."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> After Collect All, do the samples live <em>inside</em> the Project folder?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-3","title":"1.10.3 — Song_v01 never overwrite","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.3 — Song_v01 never overwrite","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> <code>v02</code> is cheap. Overwriting the only good take is not.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.2 principle 5. Manual ch. 5 walk-throughs use Save As under a new name so the previous version survives."},{"type":"prose","html":"Habit: <code>Session_v01</code>, <code>Session_v02</code>. Experiments get a new number. The four deliverable names from 0.1.3 stay distinct files."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Do you have at least <code>v01</code> sitting untouched?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-4","title":"1.10.4 — Template: named tracks, two returns, reference lane","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.4 — Template: named tracks, two returns, reference lane","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A Set that is already a band, plus one commercial listen at low volume.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Make a template Set (File → Save Live Set As Template, Manual 5) or a default Set you duplicate:"},{"type":"list","items":["Named tracks: Drums, Bass, Chords, Lead, Vocal-or-Topline, FX  ","Two return tracks: Delay, Reverb (stock devices)  ","One audio track named <code>REF</code> — a commercial song you <strong>own</strong>, low volume, for later arrangement listen (KB §1.2). Not a mix-match exam yet."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Default sounds are fine (KB §1.2)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Open a new Set from the template. Are the names already there?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-5","title":"1.10.5 — Session vs Arrangement — Tab, Back to Arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.5 — Session vs Arrangement — Tab, Back to Arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Tab switches views. Orange means Session is overriding the ruler.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §2.1 / Manual ch. 6–7:"},{"type":"table","headers":["View","Metaphor","Best for"],"rows":[["<strong>Session</strong>","Vertical clip grid","Ideas, loops, improvisation, launching"],["<strong>Arrangement</strong>","Horizontal timeline","Linear structure, long takes, mix automation"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Tab</strong> (or the view selectors) switches. Improvising in Session can be recorded into Arrangement (Arrangement Record). On a given track, a <strong>playing Session clip overrides</strong> Arrangement content until you stop the clip or press <strong>Back to Arrangement</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Back to Arrangement button lights when what you hear is not what the ruler says (Manual 7.5). That light is information, not an error."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Make the button light up on purpose, then put it out."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-6","title":"1.10.6 — Why two different songs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.6 — Why two different songs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Independent clip pools — preview of Deliverables 1 and 2.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.5: clips in Session and Arrangement <strong>exist independently</strong>. You can record a Session pass onto the ruler as a <em>document</em>. You can also write a new piece only on the ruler."},{"type":"prose","html":"This course forbids submitting that document as Deliverable 2. Song 1 is Session-native. Song 2 is a <strong>different</strong> Arrangement-native piece."},{"type":"prose","html":"Industry “sketch then polish” is allowed as <em>how you archive a jam</em>. It is not the capstone rule."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Why would dumping Session into Arrangement fail Gate E later?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-7","title":"1.10.7 — MK4 transport + Capture MIDI","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.7 — MK4 transport + Capture MIDI","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Play, stop, record, and grab the idea you already played.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §5.5: hardware <strong>Record</strong> follows the <strong>active view</strong>. Session → Session Record. Arrangement → Arrangement Record. Play / Stop / Loop are transport. Second Stop often returns to the start."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Capture MIDI</strong> (Live) stores what you just played even if you were not recording. Use it in this course as the “I had it” button. Four bars of nonsense is a legal first capture."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not turn on Live’s <strong>MPE</strong> checkbox on the MK4 port. Pads are polyphonic <strong>aftertouch</strong>. MPE on that port can monopolize channels and <strong>break</strong> channel-10 Drum Racks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Practice:</strong> New template Set. Capture 4 bars of nonsense."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Can you find those 4 bars in a Session slot?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-8","title":"1.10.8 — Clip mode first","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.8 — Clip mode first","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The MK4’s Session superpower.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §5.4 (official DAW maps):"},{"type":"table","headers":["Mode","Typical enter","Job"],"rows":[["<strong>Clip</strong>","Shift + Pad 9 (DAW) once","Launch Session clips / navigate the grid"],["Sequencer","Shift + Pad 9 again","Step-edit (later / Launchkey course)"],["<strong>Drum</strong>","Shift + Pad 10","Play Drum Rack pads (notes 36–51, ch. 10)"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Learn Clip mode first.</strong> It is Live’s superpower with Session View. Drum mode arrives when you chop in Module 1."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pads: 16 pads, 2×8, notes <strong>36–51</strong>, channel <strong>10</strong>, C1 = pad 1."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Are you in Clip mode before you hunt a synth?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-9","title":"1.10.9 — Shift-light health check","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.9 — Shift-light health check","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> DAW / Plugin / Mixer / Sends / Transport — know which layer you are on.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"The MK4’s Shift-layer lights tell you which control bank is active (DAW, Plugin, Mixer, Sends, Transport — per Novation / academy Launchkey docs). If encoders “do nothing,” you are usually on the wrong layer, not broken."},{"type":"prose","html":"Full Components / every mode = Launchkey course. Here: if transport or Clip launch fails, check the Shift lights before you reinstall anything."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Change layer on purpose. Watch the lights. Change back."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l0-10-10","title":"1.10.10 — Default sounds are fine","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.10.10 — Default sounds are fine","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Core Library first. Plugins later.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.2 principle 3. You are not missing the real DAW. Standard is a full production environment (KB §1.3). Operator and Max for Live are installed; they are <strong>not</strong> this week’s homework."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 1.10 practice:</strong> New template Set; Capture 4 bars of nonsense.   <strong>Section 1.10 checkpoint:</strong> You can find your Project folder on disk."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Part C (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beat Kitchen, <em>The Genre Landscape</em>; <em>Arrangement and Energy Management</em> (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)  ","<em>House Music Cheat Sheet</em>; <em>DNB Cheat Sheet</em>; local dubstep KB form/scenes; <em>How To Make Trap</em> / <em>How To Make Techno</em> / <em>How To Make Dubstep (UK_140)</em> for cited bands only  ","Berklee Now, “Electronic Music Genres” (2025) — DnB 160–180; dubstep 140 / 4/4  ","KB §1.2, §2.1, §5.4–5.5, §8.7  ","Live 12 Standard Manual ch. 5 (Save, Collect All and Save, Projects), ch. 6–7 (views, Back to Arrangement, independent clips)  ","Novation Launchkey MK4 DAW control / academy Launchkey KB"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
